        &lt;source_link&gt;https://teamserver-staging.contsec.com/Contrast/static/ng/index.html#/119844af-42ff-4293-b06b-81d426e9a4a9/applications/ece4f31a-8242-4921-9771-ff60c395383f/vulns/33QX-ZA6U-94XG-Q3TK&lt;/source_link&gt;
        &lt;source_mitigation_status/&gt;
        &lt;source_notes/&gt;
        &lt;source_recommendation&gt;&lt;![CDATA[There are a number of common defenses that actually offer no protection:


Only accepting POST requests. Although the canonical example uses an HTML tag (like ) to cause the victim&amp;#39;s browser
to issue a malicious request, attacker&amp;#39;s can also JavaScript to issue POST requests, including parameters, XML or JSON.
Requiring Multiple Step Transactions. Attackers can use JavaScript to submit multiple requests 
in their attack. In fact, attacker tooling makes this a point-and-click exercise.
Creating a Token in a Cookie.
Developers have sometimes responded to CSRF vulnerability reports by creating a new cookie, with a secret value, and requiring that 
with every request. This offers no protection, because the new cookie will be submitted with the attacker-generated request along with
the user&amp;#39;s original session cookies.


Every request that performs state-changing behaviors, like updating a database record or modifying a file, should have a unique
transaction token. This token can be unique to the user&amp;#39;s session, or change on every request, but it should always be passed in a 
parameter. This is what&amp;#39;s called the &amp;#34;token synchronization pattern&amp;#34;, and is the most effective, and future-safe approach.


You may notice that Contrast will also not report a URL if any of the following are true:


The developer enabled https://www.owasp.org/index.php/Category:OWASP_CSRFGuard_Project$$LINK_DELIM$$OWASP CSRFGuard to protect the given request.
The application is using an obvious CSRF token.
The &amp;#34;X-Requested-With&amp;#34; header is sent. There is no way for an attacker to control this header value without another unrelated vulnerability already being present.
There were no state-changing behaviors observed. If the request has no side effects, then the attacker most likely won&amp;#39;t have anything to gain by forging the request.
There is an established Exception for the given URL in the &amp;#34;Exceptions&amp;#34; section of the application configuration in Contrast.
The response code for a URL is not 2XX.

]]&gt;&lt;/source_recommendation&gt;
