        &lt;source_severity&gt;5&lt;/source_severity&gt;
        &lt;source_target_fix_date/&gt;
        &lt;source_vulnerability_explanation&gt;&lt;![CDATA[We observed a page which had state changing operations without a unique transaction token:]]&gt;&lt;/source_vulnerability_explanation&gt;
        &lt;source_vulnerability_summary&gt;&lt;![CDATA[An attacker could have caused a victim user to perform the behavior above against their will by tricking them into interacting with a malicious page on another site. This type of attack is called Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF).

For instance, the following image HTML could be placed in a malicious page by an attacker:

&lt;img src="https://bank.com/transferMoney?targetAccount&amp;#61;59851&amp;amp;sourceAccount&amp;#61;SAVINGS&amp;amp;amount&amp;#61;100.00" /&gt;

If the victim were to visit a malicious page containing that code, the victim&amp;#39;s browser would request the image with the URL specified, and in effect, the attacker&amp;#39;s chosen malicious request would &amp;#34;ride along with&amp;#34; the user&amp;#39;s already authenticated session. This is why some have called this vulnerability &amp;#34;Session Riding.&amp;#34;

Even though the request is for an image, it will look &amp;#34;normal&amp;#34; to the bank, and will be processed as if the user had gone through the normal page workflow of transferring money.

This is a subtle vulnerability which requires explicit protection, and developer awareness is generally very low. We strongly recommend investing in creating an architectural solution for CSRF, rather than fixing individual pages.]]&gt;&lt;/source_vulnerability_summary&gt;
